# `may` reads two ways, as permission (`the caller may pass nil`) or as
# a possibility (`the cache may be stale`). The technical-writing skill
# keeps `may` for an option a person is free to take and writes
# `might` for a possibility. The rule counts `may` before a verb or an
# adverb after any word but `you`. The fix writes `might` for a
# possibility and `can` for an ability or a permission.
#
# The guard on the next word skips the phrases other rules report:
# `may not` and `may or may not` (the may-not rule), `may want to`
# (the obligation rule), and `may possibly` and `may potentially` (the
# redundant-pair rule). The verb or adverb after `may` keeps out the
# month, which a number, a comma, or a noun follows (`in May 2026`, `the
# May release`). Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter consumes the
# word before `may` and narrows the finding back to it. A sentence that
# opens with `May` is a question or a date, so it passes. ProseCapsObligation
# also reports an uppercase `MAY`, which takes a second fix.
#
# It hit 367 lines in the kubernetes and nix.dev docs, and 17 of 20
# sampled hits stated a possibility. The other three stated an ability
# or a permission (`an implementation may represent the backend`),
# where `can` reads the same.
expr skip [not, want, possibly, potentially]
expr main <(!you may <[VERB, AUX, ADV], !@skip>), may>
let message "May for a possibility. Write might, or can for an ability."
let description "Flags may outside you may, where it usually states a possibility that might states in one reading."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "You may skip the tag."
allows "The cache might be stale after a restart."
allows "The name may not be a dot."
allows "You may want to pin the version."
allows "Version 2 shipped in May."
